menu "Super Spectral"

    config SPECTRAL_BOOT_GUARD_MS
        int "Boot guard delay before any init (ms)"
        default 3000
        range 1000 10000
        help
            Unconditional delay executed as the FIRST statement of app_main(),
            before any peripheral, PMU, display or sleep code runs (ADR 0015,
            anti-brick policy).

            The T-Watch S3 exposes no GPIO and its BOOT button is on the
            internal PCB; the only re-flash path is esptool driving the
            reset-to-download sequence over the on-chip USB-Serial-JTAG
            controller, which re-enumerates in roughly one second after each
            reset. A firmware that crashes, deep-sleeps or reconfigures
            GPIO19/20 faster than that can never be caught by `idf.py flash`.
            Three seconds beats the enumeration window with margin, so the
            host always wins the race - even in a crash loop.

            Lower bound 1000 ms is the enumeration time itself; do not set
            this below the default even for release builds. Raising it costs
            nothing but boot time.

    config SPECTRAL_DEV_SLEEP_ARMED
        bool "Allow sleep entry during development"
        default n
        help
            Every sleep entry (light or deep) is additionally gated by
            "awake >= N s AND no USB host attached" at runtime (ADR 0015).
            During development this compile-time switch keeps sleep code
            unreachable entirely: an early esp_deep_sleep_start() makes the
            USB-Serial-JTAG device disappear from the host even with the
            cable attached, and light sleep gates the USB PHY clock.

            Leave n until the rollback path and the boot-guard race have been
            tested on hardware (docs/validation/experiments, recipe 0002).
            Release builds are expected to flip this per ADR, never ad hoc.

    menu "Test hooks (never enabled in a shipped build)"

        config SPECTRAL_TEST_CRASH_AFTER_GUARD
            bool "Crash (abort) immediately after the boot guard"
            default n
            help
                Image B of docs/validation/experiments/0002: the firmware aborts on the
                first statement after the boot guard, producing a ~3 s crash loop, to
                prove that esptool still wins the download-mode race on a USB-Serial-JTAG
                -only board. Build with
                  idf.py -D SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.ci.release" \
                         -D CONFIG_SPECTRAL_TEST_CRASH_AFTER_GUARD=y build
                and flash to ota_1 with tools/flash.sh --no-arm. The pre-commit hook
                sdkconfig-invariants rejects =y in any committed sdkconfig file.

    endmenu

endmenu
